<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Cocaine use disorder, severe, with cocaine-induced psychotic disorder (F14.250)</content>. Dr. Featherstone-Ibe confirms a severe cocaine use disorder with a cocaine-induced psychotic disorder: the delusions began during heavy use, he has never had psychotic symptoms off cocaine, they are already fading, and the examination, laboratory tests and MRI show no other cause. A primary psychotic disorder would be considered again if symptoms outlast a month of abstinence. Because they persist ten days after his last use and keep him from sleeping, an antipsychotic is advised. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or of harming anyone; the safety plan is in his phone. [S]<list><item>Aripiprazole 5 mg each morning, with a plan to taper after a period of remission.</item><item>Contingency management with twice-weekly urine tests and prize draws for twelve weeks, starting today.</item><item>Weekly cognitive behavioral therapy with the clinic counselor.</item><item>Baseline fasting lipids today; hemoglobin A1c 5.5% and QTc 436 ms at intake.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Raised blood pressure (R03.0)</content>. 148/94 mmHg ten days after cocaine; still raised. [S]<list><item>Home blood pressure readings twice daily in the week before the four-week visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Financial insecurity (Z59.86)</content>. Money worries keep him awake. [S]<list><item>Meeting with the clinic social worker next week.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Cocaine use since the last visit (his own account), cravings and triggers</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No cocaine for ten days; cravings in the evenings</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Urine drug tests in contingency management, with fentanyl, and prize draws for negative results</td><td>Twice weekly for twelve weeks, then at visits</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Intake test positive for cocaine, negative for fentanyl; contingency management begins today with the first test, negative</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Psychotic symptoms, insight and sleep</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Persecutory beliefs less firmly held; no hallucinations; sleep about four hours</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antipsychotic safety: weight, blood pressure, akathisia (Barnes scale) and abnormal movements (AIMS)</td><td>Weight and akathisia at every visit while on treatment; AIMS at baseline and every six months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Baseline before aripiprazole: weight 101.0 kg, BMI 28.9, blood pressure 148/94; AIMS 0; no akathisia; QTc 436 ms at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Fasting glucose or hemoglobin A1c and lipids on an antipsychotic</td><td>Baseline, at four months, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Baseline fasting lipids today: total cholesterol 204, HDL 44, LDL 128, triglycerides 160 mg/dL; hemoglobin A1c 5.5% at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Cognitive screen (MoCA)</td><td>At baseline and again once he is well and off cocaine</td><td>Up to date</td><td>MoCA 25 at intake during symptoms and short sleep; repeat once he is well</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Brain MRI for new psychotic symptoms after age 50</td><td>Once, at presentation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No contusion, microbleeds or mass; brain volume normal for age</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure in clinic and at home</td><td>Every visit; home readings before diagnosis and after each change</td><td>Ordered</td><td>148/94 mmHg; home readings planned before the four-week visit</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide and violence risk review, safety plan and the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide or of harming anyone; no firearms; 988 saved in his phone</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Naloxone and fentanyl test strips on hand</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Naloxone and test strips at home; he knows how to use them</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression (PHQ-9) and anxiety (GAD-7)</td><td>At baseline and every four weeks while in treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 8 and GAD-7 9 at intake; repeat at four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Cognitive behavioral therapy</td><td>Weekly</td><td>Ordered</td><td>First session booked this week</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Finances, social work and taxpayer services</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Social work appointment next week</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Family involvement, only with his consent</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>He does not want family contacted for now; respected</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Addiction psychiatry consultation</td><td>At the start of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Featherstone-Ibe today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Persecutory fears gone and six or more hours of sleep. In progress; target 1969-08-10 [S]</item><item>No cocaine use, his goal, with contingency management and cognitive behavioral therapy. In progress; target 1969-10-26 [S]</item><item>Blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg. In progress; target 1969-10-26 [S]</item><item>A clear answer about his memory and thinking once he is well. In progress; target 1969-09-21 [S]</item><item>A plan for money that lets him sleep. In progress; target 1969-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: The ASAM/AAAP Clinical Practice Guideline on the Management of Stimulant Use Disorder (2023); The American Psychiatric Association Practice Guideline for the Treatment of Patients With Schizophrenia (2020); Treatment for Stimulant Use Disorders (Treatment Improvement Protocol 33, updated 2021) (2021); National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke Consensus Diagnostic Criteria for Traumatic Encephalopathy Syndrome (2021); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, hypertension and depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Four-week review in three weeks with home blood pressure readings, the PHQ-9 and GAD-7. Contingency management twice weekly and therapy weekly. Call the clinic or 988, or go to the emergency department, for any of the urgent signs on his plan. Next scheduled visit 1969-08-10: four-week review: psychotic symptoms, urine tests, blood pressure and a diagnosis of hypertension. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
